{"author":{"id":"1f592c0ac5a83ec8bdb9b8574bd8dddf2082ee3c98ad9608c7b89c82d05faf99"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791893195,"nonce":"kT/t2eAkFOcYxRufUWsAXkXot2w=","title":"Reuse native video input storage during sustained capture","message":"ChimpMonk now records 2D/3D viewports through the native H264 encoder with bounded queues and persistent Haxe/GPU readback buffers. A native 800x560 window check measured median 7.05 ms per accepted capture call (render, readback and enqueue together), 19 accepted frames over about 0.8 s. This is capture overhead, not an isolated codec benchmark.\n\nSource audit: VideoEncoder::send allocates a packed Vec and copies the frame on every submission, then Apple video_send allocates a CVPixelBuffer and copies each row. RGBA also swaps channels. Investigate directly borrowing compatible packed BGRA frame storage, retaining a reusable conversion buffer for strided/planar inputs, and CVPixelBufferPool reuse. Preserve bounded memory, owned frame lifetime, backpressure, custom layouts, timestamps, independent decoded color checks and native lifecycle tests. Measure each candidate separately before changing defaults. Report allocator/GPU costs rather than assuming the frame path is allocation-free.","files":null}]}